The double structure seen in the vertex distribution could occur for two reasons. Since each beam is composed of 56 bunches, the case could be that some bunches were mis-aligned and collided at a different places than the rest of the bunches. The second possibility is that some beam particles are present around each bunch. These residual particles could form a halo in front of and/or behind the main bunch and interact with the incoming bunch from the opposing ring, creating a non-gaussian or asymmetric distribution. In Figure 4.4 the collision vertex is presented for each bunch crossing from the data taken on day 137. The common presence of the double peak structure suggests a beam residual.

